ted. CU at Congress, I guess!?"}, "title": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "Show HN: Carton Clock – A working pendulum clock made from empty drink cartons"}, "url": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "https://www.niklasroy.com/carton-clock/"}}, "_tags": ["story", "author_r0r0", "story_50482279", "show_hn", "front_page"], "author": "r0r0", "children": [50506349, 50506589, 50506744, 50508870], "created_at": "2026-11-13T12:00:18Z", "created_at_i": 1794571218, "num_comments": 2, "objectID": "50482279", "points": 54, "story_id": 50482279, "story_text": "After my last trash clock (<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=49930439\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=49930439</a>), I wanted to see how low I could go. My latest clock is built mainly from empty drink cartons, plus a few skewers, some paper straws, a piece of string, some nuts as weights, and lots of hot glue. It has a pendulum, an escapement, and hands for seconds, minutes, and hours, and runs for about an hour.<p>The design of the gears and escapement was AI-assisted: Claude built me interactive browser tools that generate templates and 3D-printable jigs and tools.<p>I’m happy to answer your questions! And to the commenter who suggested I submit trash clock timekeeping as a talk to 40C3: I did, and it got accepted. CU at Congress, I guess!?", "title": "Show HN: Carton Clock – A working pendulum clock made from empty drink cartons", "updated_at": "2026-11-15T18:55:11Z", "url": "https://www.niklasroy.com/carton-clock/"}, {"_highlightResult": {"author": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "lumpa"}, "title": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "20 Years of Pyrolog"}, "url": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "https://pypy.org/posts/2026/11/20-years-of-pyrolog.html"}}, "_tags": ["story", "author_lumpa", "story_50467539", "front_page"], "author": "lumpa", "children": [50508602, 50508930, 505